All,

These would be for systems with PE mains and laterals.  Understand our over=
arching goal is to win the use of PVC mains and PVC laterals but when that =
is not possible this is how we would specify an all PE system.

If you think this is long and tortuous, imagine what it took to learn, conc=
eive, and detail this information.  I had to read all 32 revisions.

Overview

This specification "system" and the drawing details that should accompany i=
t are the primary deliverable of a sales effort when they are adopted in th=
e bid documents of an irrigation consultant.  When this is achieved the cha=
nces of a sale and at higher margins is increased.  However, defending won =
specs is important and challenging.

This specification system has layers with successively stronger product dif=
ferentiation that successively favor Harco commercially.  There are referen=
ces to competitive points that deserve further explanation and continuous m=
onitoring.  The better you understand the competitive concepts underpinning=
 these specifications the better you will be able to deploy them.  Note we =
have not been as effective as our competitors (Leemco, CMF, and Isco) at wi=
nning these kind of detailed specifications.  In many cases we have relied =
more on the good will of distributors or contractors to win business.  Thes=
e parallel thrusts are important and enhanced by a favorable specification =
from the owner/consultant.

Attached is a "deck" of specification module alternatives for a PE irrigati=
on system framed to provide technically accurate and complete language.  It=
's scope addresses Harco and other linked products.  It's language is also =
structured to take advantage of product differentiations offered by Harco p=
roducts which benefit the user and also provide for Harco's commercial bene=
fit and defense.  These differentiations can be explicated to the user for =
him to understand and adopt.  They offer true advantages rather than irrele=
vant differences.  Of course that can be in the eye of the beholder.  Some =
of the benefits are higher order than others.  As you go through it I think=
 most will be obvious or revealed.

Also attached is an annotation of the document that provides explanations o=
f its details and background.  This document is for internal Harco use only=
 and is not to be given to any non Harco employee.

To use this properly you would select and cull the specification deck as th=
e situation suggests to arrive at a final non-redundant and non-contradicto=
ry specification.  In many cases there will be a few alternatives from whic=
h will be selected one paragraph.  For instance, there are 4 versions of th=
e LIV specification.  You would then save the file with a file name that id=
entifies the customer.  This file should be copied to Mike Friedman and Joh=
n Riordan in order to incorporate in a database to track such things.  Ther=
e will always be a matter of what the customer actually uses.  This should =
also be sent to Mike Friedman and John Riordan.

A sample specification is attached where I made choices of alternatives.  N=
ote it reduced the number of pages from 8 to 3.  This is a very straight fo=
rward process.  The document is set up to renumber paragraphs automatically=
 when a paragraph is removed.

Commercial pressure points:


1.       Our first priority in winning a PE spec is to achieve adoption of =
the LIV. This is the commercial high ground in the effort.  If this is spec=
ified the other mainline fittings and conventional lateral fittings other t=
han service fittings are usually a given.


2.       Failing that our next priority is to win the PE ball valve spec fo=
r the lateral connections.  This also can effectively tie the other main li=
ne and lateral fittings to us.


3.       Then our priority is to win the specification of the PE fittings e=
specially the requirement for machined ends which we are best positioned to=
 serve.


4.       Gaining specs for Philmac or our fusion x acme fittings substantia=
lly enhance our revenue results.


5.       Mainline gate valves we can go either of three ways.  Flanged valv=
es with us supplying the adapters which is our best gross profit scenario. =
 MJ valves with us supplying the adapters.  AVK PE ended valves or PE Ball =
valves are the other two scenarios and dictate a purchase from Harco in the=
 irrigation market although with somewhat lower gross profit results.

Special comments on pipe:

We are no longer emphasizing the high PENT or more recently framed as the e=
xtended Ductile to Brittle transition extrapolation that has been a feature=
 of Paul Granger's specification.  This is because it disadvantages us in t=
he Northeast where Duraline has stopped making pressure pipe giving Danny Z=
alvorson/Flying W Pipe an angle that tends to draw the attention of Integri=
ty Fusion.  Any versions of this spec should be deleted.  If you have any c=
ustomers using it you need to find a way to change it.  One thing we have l=
earned recently is the fusion process degrades the capability of the materi=
al to the point there is no advantage to using the high PENT materials over=
 the lower PENT materials for purposes of gaining a more capable fusion.  S=
ince there is no performance advantage in the fusion joints the competitive=
ness of the bid is degraded with no gain in system performance.

Future changes:

Shortly the PE industry will publish guidance on exposure to Chlorine disin=
fectants requiring adjustments to these specifications.  We are currently i=
n the process of transitioning some of our material choices on this account=
.

This system will be upgraded and enhanced as we move forward.

Let me know if you have questions, insights, or identify mistakes.

J

